><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Pratctioners, researchers, coaches and athletes should be aware&#xD;of the injury profile established in this study, with particular&#xD;reference to the higher rate of BSIs in female athletes. Future&#xD;research should confirm the findings of this research in other&#xD;elite triathlete populaitons and investigate the the specific factors&#xD;that predispose and lead to BSIs occurrence in female triathletes.&#xD;Longitudinal prospective studies that incorporate both injury&#xD;occurrence and detailed training load data across the three&#xD;disciplines of short-course&#xD;triathlon are required to progress the&#xD;understanding of injury aetiology in this population.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="5" rect="595.276001,108.309891,779.276001,200.309891"
/></highlight
